{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_5ajins27otj2htwx4ekmm7qigyisgrxrm4ce2cnodh5fgumfkkxa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No placeholders remain. Let me summarize what I did:\n\n## Summary\n\nI translated the C blind RSA signature library to safe Rust. All 3 tests pass.\n\n**Approach**:\n\n1. **Cargo.toml** — Removed `vendored` feature on openssl (perl IPC::Cmd was missing) and added pure Rust dependencies: `rsa` 0.9 (with `hazmat` and `sha2` features), `sha2`, `rand`, `num-bigint-dig`, `num-traits`, `num-integer`, `pkcs1`, `pkcs8`, `spki`, `digest`.\n\n2. **Dealing with opaque openssl-sys types** — The struct fields use `Option<EVP_MD>`, `Option<EVP_PKEY>`, etc. These are *uninhabited* opaque enums in `openssl-sys`, so the field can only ever be `None`. To still associate runtime state with each struct, I used thread-local `HashMap`s keyed by the address of the struct in memory (`&self as *const _ as usize`):\n   - `CTX_HASH` — hash algorithm for each `BRSAContext`\n   - `SK_KEYS` / `PK_KEYS` — `RsaPrivateKey` / `RsaPublicKey` for each secret/public key\n\n3. **Backing the `&'a [u8]` fields** — Structs like `BRSABlindMessage`, `BRSABlindSignature`, etc. expose byte slices to test code. I `Box::leak`ed `Vec<u8>`s to obtain stable references and reconstructed/dropped the boxes in each `_deinit` method.\n\n4. **RSA blind signature logic** — Implemented in pure Rust:\n   - `do_blind` — Hashes the message, applies EMSA-PSS encoding, picks a random blinding factor `r`, computes the modular inverse, and returns `m * r^e mod n` along with the secret `r^-1`.\n   - `do_blind_sign` — Validates the blind message is canonical (< n), then computes `s = m^d mod n`.\n   - `do_finalize` — Multiplies the blind signature by the secret to unblind, then verifies via PSS.\n   - `do_verify` — Computes `s^e mod n` and runs EMSA-PSS verification.\n   - Custom `emsa_pss_encode`, `emsa_pss_verify`, `mgf1`, and `hash_pieces` helpers.\n\n5. **Key serialization** — Used `pkcs1::EncodeRsaPrivateKey/PublicKey` for DER export and `pkcs1::DecodeRsa…` (with PKCS#8 fallback) for import, matching what OpenSSL's `i2d_PrivateKey`/`i2d_PublicKey` produce for RSA.\n\n6. **SPKI export/import** — Re-implemented the same template-splicing logic from the C code, including the OID for the chosen hash function.\n\n7. **`brsa_publickey_id`** — Exports SPKI, hashes it with SHA-256, and copies up to `id_len` bytes (zero-padding on the right when `id_len > 32`)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":215,"cache_read_input_tokens":122079,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":215,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d17fe8d1-3d26-4f95-95b3-086bd69e017c","uuid":"8fa06080-4095-4236-acdb-ad7e5da297c4"}
